  "_legend": "Stonewake climbs a quarried mountainside in two steps. The lower city is a grid: gravel lanes, two laid-stone thoroughfares (Kerb Street east to west, the ramp road north to south), and blocks of brick terraces between them. The museum takes the whole east end of the north block; the Meridian Foundation office faces it across the square in glass and steel. Above the retaining wall is the upper terrace -- three worked bays, the cutting shed, the powder store, the quarry office, the winding house, and the Stone Hall on its own bench with its back in the rock.",
  "_plan": "Scale is carried by buildings that never open. Bothies, stores and shuttered terraces put thirteen doors among something over fifty roofs, which is what makes the place read as a city rather than as a village with more houses. Two ways up to the terrace -- the ramp road and the haul road -- plus the old adit at the east end, so the top of the city is never one corridor. The west working is stopped by a fallen stone, and Shoulder, which this Hall itself teaches, is what opens it."
}
